---
description: Application layer rules — use cases, commands/queries, ports, transactions.
globs: **/application/**/*.java
alwaysApply: false
---

# Application layer

- One class per use case. Interface `<Verb><Noun>UseCase` (input port) + implementation `<Verb><Noun>Service`.
- Commands and queries are immutable records with primitive/VO fields; no domain objects in commands. Results are small DTO records or ids.
- A use case orchestrates only: load aggregate via port → call domain behavior → save → publish events. Business rules live in the domain, never here.
- Transaction boundary = one use case = one aggregate mutated. Cross-aggregate consistency via domain events (outbox pattern in infra), not one big transaction.
- Depends on `domain` only. Allowed Spring: `@Transactional`, optionally `@Service`. Constructor injection.
- Output ports (interfaces) for anything external: `OrderRepository`, `PaymentGateway`, `DomainEventPublisher`, `Clock`. Never call adapters directly.
- Queries (CQRS-lite): read-only, may use dedicated read ports/DTOs and bypass aggregates. Never mutate.
- Inject `java.time.Clock` / an id generator port; never call `Instant.now()`/`UUID.randomUUID()` inline.
- Application exceptions (`OrderNotFoundException`) are specific; let domain exceptions propagate.
- Tests: JUnit + in-memory fakes for ports (`InMemoryOrderRepository`); Mockito only for external side effects. No Spring context.
